# Text-to-Speech (TTS)

CU "Listen" buttons read out content via the browser **Web Speech API** (`SpeechSynthesis`). All voice logic lives in **one** place: `web-viewer-sample-main/src/services/speechSynthesis.ts`. Per-character voice data lives in **`interactions.json`** on the Kit side — the frontend never picks a default character voice.

## Two axes, kept separate

| Axis | Driven by | Why |
|------|-----------|-----|
| **Voice language** (e.g. `fr-FR`, `sv-SE`) | The language of the *text* being spoken | WCAG 3.1.2 — words must be pronounced with the right phonetic rules. |
| **Voice character** (gender, rate, pitch, name hint) | `AvatarVoiceProfile` from Kit | Keeps a given avatar consistent across UI languages (Red sounds female in en *and* sv). |

Voice gender is a **preference**, not a hard filter — many OSes only ship one voice per locale.

## Key files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `services/speechSynthesis.ts` | `speakText()`, `pickVoice()`, `parseVoiceProfile()`, `ttsLanguageFor()`, `AvatarVoiceProfile`, `FEMALE_/MALE_/NEUTRAL_VOICE_PROFILE` constants |
| `services/aiLanguage.ts` | `aiLanguageFor()` — UI-lang → AI-supported lang (en/sv only) for AI request payloads *and* for tagging incoming AI replies |
| `kit-app-template-main/source/data/interactions.json` | Source of truth for avatar voice data: `npcConfig.voice` (NPCs) and `ui.voice` (interaction boxes) |
| `streaming/overlays/avatarChat/AvatarChatOverlay.tsx` | Avatar chat "Listen" — uses `config.voiceProfile` + per-message `m.lang` |
| `streaming/overlays/npcBubble/NpcBubbleOverlay.tsx` | NPC speech bubble "Listen" — uses `cfg.voiceProfile` + UI lang |
| `streaming/overlays/interactionBoxes/InteractionBoxesOverlay.tsx` | Interaction-box "Listen" — uses `parseVoiceProfile(ui?.voice)` + UI lang |
| `streaming/contexts/ChatContext.tsx` | Coerces `payload.voice` from Kit's `avatarChat.open` into a profile via `parseVoiceProfile` |
| `streaming/StreamOnlyWindow.tsx` | Tags incoming `ai.agent.response` messages with `lang: aiLanguageFor(i18n.language)` so the listen button uses the right voice |

## Voice data on Kit side

Voice is a **string** (gender shorthand) or **object** (full descriptor) on the Kit interaction entry. The frontend `parseVoiceProfile` accepts both. New avatars opt in by shipping data — never by hardcoding in the frontend.

```jsonc
// NPC: simple gender shorthand
"npcConfig": {
  "avatarId": "red",
  "avatarName": "Red",
  "voice": "female",          // "female" | "male" | "any"
  ...
}

// Or full descriptor for custom rate/pitch/branded voice:
"npcConfig": {
  "voice": { "gender": "male", "rate": 0.95, "pitch": 0.9, "voiceNameHints": "alex|daniel" }
}

// Interaction box (speechBubbleBottom etc.) — same shapes on `ui.voice`:
"behaviors": [
  { "type": "projectable", "ui": { "style": "speechBubbleBottom", "title": "...", "voice": "male" } }
]
```

The Kit interactions extension forwards the entire `npcConfig` (and `ui`) verbatim to the frontend, so any new key shows up automatically — no Kit-side schema changes needed.

## Language of the text vs language of the UI

Most spoken text is properly localized via i18n (`t(key)`) or `interactions.json` `title_<lang>` fields, so **the text language matches the UI language** and the caller just passes `i18n.language` to `speakText`.

The **one exception** is AI chat replies: the AI backend only supports `en`/`sv`. In a French/Spanish UI, `ChatContext` sends the request with `language: aiLanguageFor(i18n.language) === 'en'`, the AI replies in English, and we tag the message:

```typescript
// StreamOnlyWindow.tsx — `ai.agent.response` handler
const lang = String(p.language ?? aiLanguageFor(i18n.language));
chat.conversation.addMessage(avatarId, { role: 'assistant', text, lang });
```

`AvatarChatOverlay.handleListenClick` then reads `m.lang ?? i18n.language` so the English text gets an English voice instead of being mangled by a French TTS engine.

`AvatarChatMessage` carries an optional `lang?: string` field for exactly this purpose. User-typed messages, i18n greetings, quick-action labels, error strings — none set `lang` because they're already in the UI language.

## Adding a "Listen" button to a new component

1. Import the shared utility, never re-implement:
   ```typescript
   import { speakText, type SpeechController } from '../../../services/speechSynthesis';
   ```
2. Hold the controller in a ref so you can cancel:
   ```typescript
   const ttsCtrl = useRef<SpeechController | null>(null);
   useEffect(() => () => ttsCtrl.current?.cancel(), []); // cancel on unmount
   ```
3. Toggle on click:
   ```typescript
   ttsCtrl.current = speakText(text, {
     uiLang: messageLang ?? i18n.language,  // language of the *text*
     voiceProfile: cfg.voiceProfile,        // from Kit, already parsed
     onStart: () => setPlaying(true),
     onEnd:   () => setPlaying(false),
     onError: () => setPlaying(false),
   });
   ```
4. WCAG: button needs `aria-label={t('common.listen')}` / `t('common.stopListening')` and `aria-pressed={playing}`. See `wcag-accessibility.mdc`.

## Adding a new avatar with a voice

Frontend changes: **none** for voice. Kit changes:

1. Add the NPC entry to `interactions.json` with a `voice` key in its `npcConfig`.
2. Add the i18n key for the avatar's greeting / quick actions in `src/i18n/locales/*.json`.

That's it — `parseVoiceProfile` reads the `voice` field on the `avatarChat.open` payload and the listen button picks the right voice automatically.

## Pitfalls

- **Don't hardcode `FEMALE_/MALE_VOICE_PROFILE` in components or contexts** — those constants are TTS-implementation details (rate/pitch/name-hint defaults), not data about *who* has what voice. Always go through `parseVoiceProfile(<kit data>)`.
- **Don't add new gender allowlists in `ttsLanguageFor`** — every UI language should get its own voice (the OS decides what's installed); the only "force-to-en" mapping lives in `aiLanguageFor`, scoped to AI request payloads and AI reply tagging.
- **Voice list is async on first load** — `SpeechSynthesis.getVoices()` may return `[]` on the very first call. `pickVoice` returns `null` in that case and `speakText` falls back to the OS default; subsequent clicks pick correctly.
- **Always cancel on unmount** — utterances continue speaking after the component unmounts unless you call `controller.cancel()` in a cleanup effect.
